    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Ectopic lymph node was considered — “Based on clinical and radiographic findings, a provisional diagnosis of ectopic lymph node and lipoma was made.”\n2. Lipoma was considered — “Based on clinical and radiographic findings, a provisional diagnosis of ectopic lymph node and lipoma was made.”\n3. Keloid was included in the differential — “The differential diagnosis includes keloid…”\n4. Mucocele was included in the differential — “The differential diagnosis includes keloid, mucocele, salivary gland tumour…”\n5. Salivary gland tumour (e.g., canalicular adenoma) was included — “The differential diagnosis includes…salivary gland tumour (such as canalicular adenoma arising from a minor salivary gland in the lip)…”\n6. Neurilemmoma was included in the differential — “The differential diagnosis includes…neurilemmoma…”\n7. Neurofibroma was included in the differential — “The differential diagnosis includes…neurofibroma (which might mimic angioleiomyoma clinically but has different IHC features)…”\n8. Granular cell tumour was included in the differential — “The differential diagnosis includes…granular cell tumour.”\n9. Angiomyolipoma was considered as a histologic mimic — “angiomyoma may exhibit morphological similarities with various benign and malignant soft tissue tumors, such as angiomyolipoma…”\n10. Myopericytoma was considered as a histologic mimic — “angiomyoma may exhibit morphological similarities with various benign and malignant soft tissue tumors, such as…myopericytoma…”\n11. Leiomyosarcoma was considered as a histologic mimic — “angiomyoma may exhibit morphological similarities with various benign and malignant soft tissue tumors, such as…leiomyosarcoma.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\nDiagnosis: Lipoma\nEvidence: The patient presents with a 2-month history of a swelling in her right cheek associated with severe pain. The intraoral examination reveals a solitary, mobile, 2 cm × 2 cm submucosal mass with ill-defined borders and a smooth mucosal surface. Ultrasonography shows a well-defined, hypoechoic lesion in the right cheek region. These findings are consistent with a lipoma, which is a common benign tumor composed of fat tissue.\nEvidence: The patient presents with a 2-month history of a swelling in her right cheek associated with severe pain. The swelling is described as a solitary, mobile, 2 cm × 2 cm submucosal mass with ill-defined borders and a smooth mucosal surface. Ultrasonography shows a well-defined, hypoechoic lesion in the right cheek region. These findings are consistent with a lipoma, which is a common benign tumor composed of fat tissue.\nEvidence: The patient's presentation of a solitary, mobile, 2 cm × 2 cm submucosal mass with ill-defined borders and a smooth mucosal surface on the right buccal mucosa is consistent with a lipoma. Ultrasonography showing a well-defined, hypoechoic lesion in the right cheek region also supports this diagnosis.\nDiagnosis: Ectopic lymph node\nEvidence: The patient's symptoms and examination findings, including the presence of a submucosal mass with ill-defined borders, could also be consistent with an ectopic lymph node. Ectopic lymph nodes are abnormal lymphoid tissue that can be found in various locations, including the oral cavity.\nEvidence: The patient's presentation of a swelling in her right cheek with severe pain, along with ultrasonography findings of a well-defined, hypoechoic lesion, could also be consistent with an ectopic lymph node. Ectopic lymph nodes are abnormal collections of lymphoid tissue that can occur in various locations, including the cheek.\nEvidence: The patient's symptoms and examination findings, including a diffuse extraoral swelling over the right cheek and a submucosal mass, could be indicative of an ectopic lymph node. However, the absence of palpable lymphadenopathy and the presence of a well-defined, hypoechoic lesion on ultrasonography make this diagnosis less likely.\n\nAnalysis of Groundtruth Diagnostic Reasons vs. Predicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n\nFor Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 1: \"Ectopic lymph node was considered — “Based on clinical and radiographic findings, a provisional diagnosis of ectopic lymph node and lipoma was made.”\"\n- Potential match 1: \"The patient's symptoms and examination findings, including the presence of a submucosal mass with ill-defined borders, could also be consistent with an ectopic lymph node. Ectopic lymph nodes are abnormal lymphoid tissue that can be found in various locations, including the oral cavity.\"\n    - Pros: Explicitly mentions \"ectopic lymph node\" and states that findings \"could also be consistent\" with it, indicating it was considered.\n    - Cons: None.\n- Potential match 2: \"The patient's presentation of a swelling in her right cheek with severe pain, along with ultrasonography findings of a well-defined, hypoechoic lesion, could also be consistent with an ectopic lymph node. Ectopic lymph nodes are abnormal collections of lymphoid tissue that can occur in various locations, including the cheek.\"\n    - Pros: Explicitly mentions \"ectopic lymph node\" and states that findings \"could also be consistent\" with it, indicating it was considered.\n    - Cons: None.\n- Potential match 3: \"The patient's symptoms and examination findings, including a diffuse extraoral swelling over the right cheek and a submucosal mass, could be indicative of an ectopic lymph node. However, the absence of palpable lymphadenopathy and the presence of a well-defined, hypoechoic lesion on ultrasonography make this diagnosis less likely.\"\n    - Pros: Explicitly mentions \"ectopic lymph node\" and states that findings \"could be indicative\" of it, indicating it was considered.\n    - Cons: None.\n\nFor Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 2: \"Lipoma was considered — “Based on clinical and radiographic findings, a provisional diagnosis of ectopic lymph node and lipoma was made.”\"\n- Potential m