  "a7ebc5109c3aaa4880601ed465fe0275f940ddf640e75987f3c2ca895f2a8fe1": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Connective tissue– or vasculitis‐associated ILD was excluded — “Connective tissue disease‐associated interstitial lung disease (ILD) and vasculitis‐associated ILD were deemed unlikely based on the negative autoantibodies results in serological testing…”\n2. Hypersensitivity pneumonitis was ruled out — “hypersensitivity pneumonitis was considered unlikely due to the absence of any identifiable history of antigen exposure.”\n3. Bacterial pneumonia was considered and excluded when antibiotics failed — “the patient was subsequently treated with lascufloxacin, followed by tazobactam/ceftolozane, but showed no improvement in oxygen saturation and worsening respiratory status…”\n4. SARS‐CoV‐2 infection was excluded — “SARS‐CoV‐2 polymerase chain reaction were negative.”\n5. Malignant infiltration was ruled out by biopsy — “Pathological examination of the trans‐bronchial lung biopsy specimen revealed no malignant findings…”\n6. Infection was further excluded by negative BAL cultures — “culture tests of the lower respiratory tract specimens obtained during bronchoscopy were negative for pathogens.”\n7. ICI-induced pneumonitis was suspected based on timing and imaging — “Based on these clinical findings, ICI‐induced pneumonitis (Grade 3) was suspected.”\n8. Steroid refractoriness was established by persistent infiltrates and worsened oxygen requirement despite two high‐dose steroid courses — “radiographic findings taken after these treatments showed a persistent infiltrative shadow, and the patient required increased oxygen support, with the flow rate rising from 2 to 4 L/min,” and “no significant changes in the respiratory status of the patient.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. The patient has a history of epithelioid pleural mesothelioma and was treated with ipilimumab and nivolumab, which are known to cause pneumonitis as a side effect. The patient's symptoms, such as fever and dyspnea, and laboratory findings, such as elevated C-reactive protein and surfactant protein D levels, are consistent with pneumonitis. The patient's lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT also support this diagnosis.\n2. The patient was treated with ipilimumab and nivolumab, which are known to cause pneumonitis. The patient's symptoms, such as fever and dyspnea, and laboratory findings, such as elevated C-reactive protein, are consistent with pneumonitis. The patient's lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT also support this diagnosis.\n3. The patient was treated with ipilimumab and nivolumab, and after eight courses, follow-up chest CT showed new bilateral infiltrative shadows, which is consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. The patient also reported fever and dyspnea, and laboratory tests revealed elevated C-reactive protein and surfactant protein D levels, which further supports this diagnosis.\n4. The patient was diagnosed with grade 3 ICI-induced pneumonitis and was treated with methylprednisolone and prednisolone, but showed no clinical improvement, indicating steroid-refractory ICI-induced pneumonitis.\n\nComparison:\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 1: Connective tissue– or vasculitis‐associated ILD was excluded — “Connective tissue disease‐associated interstitial lung disease (ILD) and vasculitis‐associated ILD were deemed unlikely based on the negative autoantibodies results in serological testing…”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. The predicted reasons focus on supporting the diagnosis of ICI-induced pneumonitis and steroid refractoriness, not on ruling out other conditions based on specific serological tests.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 2: Hypersensitivity pneumonitis was ruled out — “hypersensitivity pneumonitis was considered unlikely due to the absence of any identifiable history of antigen exposure.”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. Similar to reason 1, the predicted reasons do not mention ruling out hypersensitivity pneumonitis.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 3: Bacterial pneumonia was considered and excluded when antibiotics failed — “the patient was subsequently treated with lascufloxacin, followed by tazobactam/ceftolozane, but showed no improvement in oxygen saturation and worsening respiratory status…”\n- Potential match 1: \"The patient's lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT also support this diagnosis.\" (from Predicted Reason 1)\n    - Pros: Explicitly mentions \"lack of response to antibiotics,\" which directly aligns with the groundtruth.\n    - Cons: Does not specify the types of antibiotics or the worsening respiratory status as detailed in the groundtruth.\n- Potential match 2: \"The patient's lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT also support this diagnosis.\" (from Predicted Reason 2)\n    - Pros: Identical to Potential match 1, directly aligns with the groundtruth's mention of \"lack of response to antibiotics.\"\n    - Cons: Same as Potential match 1.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 4: SARS‐CoV‐2 infection was excluded — “SARS‐CoV‐2 polymerase chain reaction were negative.”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. The predicted reasons do not mention SARS-CoV-2 exclusion.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 5: Malignant infiltration was ruled out by biopsy — “Pathological examination of the trans‐bronchial lung biopsy specimen revealed no malignant findings…”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. The predicted reasons do not mention biopsy findings or ruling out malignancy.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 6: Infection was further excluded by negative BAL cultures — “culture tests of the lower respiratory tract specimens obtained during bronchoscopy were negative for pathogens.”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. While Predicted Reasons 1 and 2 mention \"lack of response to antibiotics,\" they do not specifically refer to negative BAL cultures as a means of excluding infection.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 7: ICI-induced pneumonitis was suspected based on timing and imaging — “Based on these clinical findings, ICI‐induced pneumonitis (Grade 3) was suspected.”\n- Potential match 1: \"The patient has a history of epithelioid pleural mesothelioma and was treated with ipilimumab and nivolumab, which are known to cause pneumonitis as a side effect. The patient's symptoms, such as fever and dyspnea, and laboratory findings, such as elevated C-reactive protein and surfactant protein D levels, are consistent with pneumonitis. The patient's lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT also support this diagnosis.\" (from Predicted Reason 1)\n    - Pros: Mentions ICI treatment (ipilimumab and nivolumab) as a cause of pneumonitis, symptoms consistent with pneumonitis, and bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT, all contributing to the suspicion.\n    - Cons: The groundtruth specifically mentions \"timing and imaging\" as the basis for suspicion. This predicted reason covers imaging (\"bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT\") and implicitly timing (treatment with ICI).\n- Potential match 2: \"The patient was treated with ipilimumab and nivolumab, which are known to cause pneumonitis. The patient's symptoms, such as fever and dyspnea, and laboratory findings, such as elevated C-reactive protein, are consistent with pneumonitis. The patient's lack of response to antibiotics and the presence of bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT also support this diagnosis.\" (from Predicted Reason 2)\n    - Pros: Similar to Potential match 1, it links ICI treatment to pneumonitis, mentions symptoms, and imaging findings (bilateral infiltrative shadows on chest CT).\n    - Cons: Same as Potential match 1.\n- Potential match 3: \"The patient was treated with ipilimumab and nivolumab, and after eight courses, follow-up chest CT showed new bilateral infiltrative shadows, which is consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis. The patient also reported fever and dyspnea, and laboratory tests revealed elevated C-reactive protein and surfactant protein D levels, which further supports this diagnosis.\" (from Predicted Reason 3)\n    - Pros: Explicitly mentions \"treated with ipilimumab and nivolumab,\" \"after eight courses\" (timing), and \"follow-up chest CT showed new bilateral infiltrative shadows\" (imaging), directly aligning with the groundtruth's \"timing and imaging.\" It also states \"consistent with ICI-induced pneumonitis.\"\n    - Cons: None. This is a very strong match.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 8: Steroid refractoriness was established by persistent infiltrates and worsened oxygen requirement despite two high‐dose steroid courses — “radiographic findings taken after these treatments showed a persistent infiltrative shadow, and the patient required increased oxygen support, with the flow rate rising from 2 to 4 L/min,” and “no significant changes in the respiratory status of the patient.”\n- Potential match 1: \"The patient was diagnosed with grade 3 ICI-induced pneumonitis and was treated with methylprednisolone and prednisolone, but showed no clinical improvement, indicating steroid-refractory ICI-induced pneumonitis.\" (from Predicted Reason 4)\n    - Pros: Explicitly states \"treated with methylprednisolone and prednisolone\" (steroid courses) and \"showed no clinical improvement, indicating steroid-refractory ICI-induced pneumonitis.\" This directly addresses the concept of steroid refractoriness.\n    - Cons: Does not explicitly mention \"persistent infiltrates\" or \"worsened oxygen requirement\" as the groundtruth does, but \"no clinical improvement\" implies a lack of resolution of the underlying issues.\n\nSummary of final matching decisions:\n- Groundtruth 1: No mat