y support this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: This statement directly mentions \"infected hematometra\" and \"ultrasound findings of a hypoechoic mass within the uterine cavity,\" which is a direct match.\n    - Cons: None.\n- Potential match 3: \"The patient's symptoms of brown-yellowish vaginal discharge, fluctuating lower abdominal pain, fever, and progressive abdominal enlargement are consistent with an infected hematometra. The transabdominal ultrasound and abdominal and pelvic computed tomography findings of a hypoechoic mass within the uterine cavity and a multilocular cystic mass with thick septa and enhancing solid components support this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: This statement directly mentions \"infected hematometra\" and \"hypoechoic mass within the uterine cavity,\" which is a direct match.\n    - Cons: None.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 4: Consideration of tubo-ovarian abscess — “CT scan revealed a cystic multilocular left adnexa lesion with thick septa and solid intra-lesion component suspected of tubo-ovarian abscess.”\n- Potential match 1: \"The patient's symptoms of brown-yellowish vaginal discharge, fluctuating lower abdominal pain, fever, and progressive abdominal enlargement are consistent with an infected hematometra. The transabdominal ultrasound and abdominal and pelvic computed tomography findings of a hypoechoic mass within the uterine cavity and a multilocular cystic mass with thick septa and enhancing solid components support this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: This statement mentions \"multilocular cystic mass with thick septa and enhancing solid components\" from CT findings, which is very similar to the description of a tubo-ovarian abscess in the ground truth. While it attributes these findings to infected hematometra, the description of the mass itself is a strong match for the imaging characteristics of a tubo-ovarian abscess.\n    - Cons: It does not explicitly name \"tubo-ovarian abscess\" as a diagnosis, but rather uses the imaging fi