ence of a large, fluid-filled lesion and the elevation of tumor markers, such as HE4 and LDH, support this diagnosis.\n\nAnalysis of potential matches:\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 1: Functional ovarian cyst is unlikely given its persistence and large size over two years — “The patient observed a gradual abdominal enlargement for two years before hospitalization.”\n- Potential match 1 (Predicted): The patient's symptoms of gradually progressive abdominal distension and the presence of a large, fluid-filled lesion in the pelvis and mid-abdomen, as seen on the CT scan, are consistent with a mucinous ovarian tumor. The tumor markers, including HE4 and LDH, are also elevated, which supports this diagnosis.\n    - Pros: Mentions \"gradually progressive abdominal distension\" which aligns with \"gradual abdominal enlargement for two years\". It also mentions \"large, fluid-filled lesion\".\n    - Cons: This statement supports a mucinous ovarian tumor, not the unlikelihood of a functional cyst. It doesn't directly address the persistence or the unlikelihood of a functional cyst.\n- Potential match 2 (Predicted): The patient's symptoms and imaging findings are also consistent with a serous ovarian tumor. The presence of a large, fluid-filled lesion and the elevation of tumor markers, such as HE4 and LDH, support this diagnosis.\n    - Pros: Mentions \"symptoms and imaging findings\" and \"large, fluid-filled lesion\".\n    - Cons: Similar to the previous, this supports a serous ovarian tumor, not the unlikelihood of a functional cyst. It doesn't directly address the persistence or the unlikelihood of a functional cyst.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 2: Germ cell tumor (e.g., yolk sac tumor, dysgerminoma) is less probable with normal AFP and β-hCG levels — “alpha-fetoprotein was 4.54 ng/mL... B-HCG was <0.200 mIU/mL.”\n- No direct match in predicted reasons. The predicted reasons mention \"tumor markers\" in a general sense (HE4 and LDH), but do not specifically refer 