  "d10c36dd65fbe41224974f2c7934c6ddf26abe840bdd14d2d9721c6855972f52": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. The eruption began shortly after ciprofloxacin exposure — “After 2 days of taking Ciprofloxacin, he started having generalized pruritus which progressed to generalized rashes filled with fluids.”\n2. The extent of skin and mucosal involvement meets criteria for TEN rather than milder reactions — “involving more than 30% of the body surface area, sometimes as much as 100% with at least two mucosal involvement.”\n3. Histopathology confirms epidermal necrolysis rather than other bullous diseases — “The impression was consistent with epidermal necrolysis secondary to ciprofloxacin.”\n4. Biopsy and clinical features rule out mimics such as SSSS, pemphigus/pemphigoid, AGEP, and GVHD — “The usual mimics are Staphylococcal Skin Scalded Syndrome (SSSS), Drug‐Induced Pemphigoid and Pemphigus…Acute Graft vs Host disease. In our case, the biopsy report and clinical presentation of the patient were consistent with the diagnosis of TEN.”\n5. Obstructive biliary pathology is excluded as a cause of jaundice — “The ultrasonography (abdomen and pelvis) showed a normal hepatobiliary scan.”\n6. Necrotizing fasciitis is unlikely given absence of severe localized pain, crepitus, edema, necrosis, or malodorous discharge — “Necrotizing fasciitis…can be ruled out with the absence of local symptoms and signs like severe pain, crepitus, edema, skin necrosis, and foul-smelling exudates.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n- The patient presented with generalized painful fluid-filled skin lesions, which could be indicative of Stevens-Johnson syndrome, a severe skin and mucous membrane disorder.\n- The patient developed generalized pruritus that progressed to fluid-filled rashes after starting ciprofloxacin, which is a common cause of Stevens-Johnson syndrome. The patient's symptoms, including painful fluid-filled skin lesions, yellow scleral discoloration, and fever, are consistent with this diagnosis.\n- The patient prese