    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Considered meningioma because the lesion was dural-based with bone invasion and vascular supply by the middle meningeal artery — “Thus, a clinical diagnosis of osteoblastic meningioma was made.”\n2. Considered primary lymphoma as part of the differential for a dural mass — “The differential diagnosis included primary lymphoma” — but histology revealed spindle cell rather than lymphoid proliferation.\n3. Considered metastasis given the skull-invading mass — “The differential diagnosis included ... metastasis” — but staging CT and PET scans showed no extracranial involvement: “Staging CT scan of the lung and abdomen and whole body positron emission tomography (PET) scan were negative for extra cranial involvement.”\n4. Considered sarcoma due to spindle cell morphology — “The differential diagnosis included ... sarcoma” — and immunohistochemical positivity for α-smooth muscle actin, vimentin, and desmin supported smooth muscle differentiation: “Histology showed a malignant spindle cell neoplasm ... with positive immunostaining for α-smooth muscle actin, vimentin, and desmin.”\n5. Confirmed high-grade leiomyosarcoma based on smooth muscle immunophenotype and malignant spindle cells — “Pathological interpretation was a malignant spindle cell neoplasm consistent with high-grade LMS.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. The patient presented with a rapidly enlarging right parietal mass behind the ear, and imaging studies revealed a 5-cm hyperdense meningeal mass with parietal bone invasion. The clinical diagnosis of osteoblastic meningioma was made, and the differential diagnosis included primary lymphoma, metastasis, and sarcoma.\n2. The patient's clinical presentation and imaging findings, including a hyperdense meningeal mass with parietal bone invasion and heterogeneous enhancement on MRI, are consistent with a meningioma. However, the histological findings of a malignant spindle cell neoplasm w