    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Primary colorectal adenocarcinoma — “primary colorectal tumours tend to demonstrate the opposite pattern being positive for CDX2 and negative for TTF1…”\n2. Primary gastrointestinal neuroendocrine tumour — “Although neuroendocrine tumours can arise in any body region 95% of all small cell carcinomas are lung origin…”\n3. Metastatic Merkel cell carcinoma — “Metastatic Merkel cell carcinoma is considered less likely as there is no cytoplasmic CK20 staining.”\n4. Metastatic small cell carcinoma of lung — “positive immunostaining for TTF1 with negative staining for Caudal-type Homeobox 2 (CDX2) lead us to suspect a lung origin tumour…”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. The patient presented with acute right lower-quadrant abdominal pain, fever, hypotension, and tachycardia, which are consistent with acute appendicitis. The contrast-enhanced CT of the abdomen showed a perforated appendix with a collection and surrounding fat stranding, confirming the diagnosis.\n2. The patient's symptoms of abdominal pain and the presence of peritonism, combined with the history of heavy cigarette smoking and the presence of mural thickening of the cecum and sigmoid colon, could be indicative of perforated diverticulitis.\n3. The patient's symptoms and CT findings could also be consistent with perforated diverticulitis, particularly given the presence of mural thickening of the cecum and sigmoid colon.\n4. The patient's symptoms and CT findings could also be consistent with perforated diverticulitis, especially given the presence of mural thickening of the cecum and sigmoid colon. However, the presence of a perforated appendix makes acute appendicitis a more likely diagnosis.\n\nAnalysis of potential matches:\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 1: Primary colorectal adenocarcinoma — “primary colorectal tumours tend to demonstrate the opposite pattern being positive for CDX2 and negative for TTF1…”\n- This statement