: Mentions \"an anomalous band in the right orbit on MRI,\" which could be considered a type of orbital lesion.\n    - Cons: This statement is more focused on the diagnostic conclusion of Orbital Pulley Disorder and less on the detailed description of the lesion's characteristics (enhancement, signal intensity) as provided in the groundtruth. The groundtruth is very specific about the imaging characteristics.\n\nFor Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 5: Intracranial mass lesion — “There were no suprasellar or parasellar mass lesions.”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. The predicted reasons do not discuss intracranial findings or the absence of suprasellar/parasellar mass lesions.\n\nFor Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 6: Congenital syndromes (Duane syndrome, Brown’s syndrome, CFEOM) — “Clinical manifestation of an accessory extraocular muscle frequently occurs in childhood, along with numerous other congenital causes, such as Duane syndrome, Brown’s syndrome, and CFEOM.”\n- No direct match in Predicted Diagnostic Reasons. The predicted reasons do not mention congenital syndromes or accessory extraocular muscles in the context of ruling out other causes. They focus on the current findings and symptoms.\n\nSummary of final matching decisions:\n- Groundtruth 1: No match.\n- Groundtruth 2: No match.\n- Groundtruth 3: No match.\n- Groundtruth 4: Predicted Diagnostic Reason 1 is the best match because it directly describes the \"right orbital linear soft-tissue lesion\" and its imaging characteristics (enhancement, signal intensity following extraocular muscle), which is the essence of the groundtruth statement about an orbital mass. Predicted Diagnostic Reason 2 is also a strong match as it contains the same descriptive elements. Predicted Diagnostic Reason 3 is less specific about the imaging characteristics of the lesion itself.\n- Groundtruth 5: No match.\n- Groundtruth 6: No match.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": [\n      \"The patient has a history of long-standing diplopia and an anomalous band in the right orbit, which was surgically excised without improvement in motility. The current MRI shows a right orbital linear soft-tissue lesion medial to the medial rectus muscle, parallel to it, with enhancement and signal-intensity characteristics identical to those of extraocular muscle.\",\n      \"The patient has a history of long-standing diplopia and an anomalous band in the right orbit, which was surgically excised without improvement in motility. The current MRI shows a right orbital linear soft-tissue lesion medial to the medial rectus muscle, parallel to it, with enhancement and signal-intensity characteristics identical to those of extraocular muscle. This suggests a possible orbital pulley disorder.\"\n    ],\n    \"5\": [],\n    \"6\": []\n  }\n}\n```\n</diagnostic_comparison>",
