tic Reason 2: Hemangiomatosis or lymphangiomatosis was considered but excluded because “there is no abnormal vascular image suggesting a localized gigantism involving the bone structure and surrounding tissue…,” indicating absence of vascular channels.\n\nPotential matches from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n- None of the predicted statements mention hemangiomatosis, lymphangiomatosis, or the absence of abnormal vascular images.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 3: Simple lipoma or ganglion cyst was considered but excluded because pathological examination later showed “a fatty tissue tumor which was limited by a septa-like connective tissue capsule, and there were several fibrotic blood vessels and fibrous connective tissue between the fatty tissue suggesting a lipofibroma,” rather than a unilocular cyst or simple lipoma.\n\nPotential matches from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n- None of the predicted statements mention simple lipoma, ganglion cyst, or the specific pathological findings described. The predicted statements focus on clinical symptoms and CT findings, not detailed pathology for exclusion of these specific conditions.\n\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reason 4: Soft tissue sarcoma was considered in light of progressive soft-tissue enlargement but was excluded by histopathology showing benign fibrofatty proliferation: “suggesting a lipofibroma” without atypia.\n\nPotential matches from Predicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n- None of the predicted statements mention soft tissue sarcoma or histopathology findings that exclude it. The predicted statements focus on supporting macrodactyly or lipomatosis of nerve, not excluding other conditions based on pathology.\n\nSummary of final matching decisions:\n\nFor Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 1: The predicted statement \"The patient's symptoms of a progressively enlarging finger since birth, along with the CT scan showing overgrowth of the phalanges and surrounding soft tissue, are consistent with macrodactyly. This condition is characterized by an abnormal enlargement of one or more fingers or toes, often due to an overgrowth of bone and soft tissue.\" is the closest match. While the groundtruth uses this information to *exclude* a condition, the predicted statement uses the *same factual information* (CT scan showing overgrowth of phalanges and soft tissue, indicating bony involvement) to support a diagnosis. The core observation about bony involvement is present in both.\n\nFor Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 2: No direct match. The predicted reasons do not discuss vascular imaging or the exclusion of hemangiomatosis/lymphangiomatosis.\n\nFor Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 3: No direct match. The predicted reasons do not discuss the exclusion of simple lipoma or ganglion cyst based on pathological findings.\n\nFor Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 4: No direct match. The predicted reasons do not discuss the exclusion of soft tissue sarcoma based on histopathology.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [\n      \"The patient's symptoms of a progressively enlarging finger since birth, along with the CT scan showing overgrowth of the phalanges and surrounding soft tissue, are consistent with macrodactyly. This condition is characterized by an abnormal enlargement of one or more fingers or toes, often due to an overgrowth of bone and soft tissue.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": []\n  }\n}\n```\n</diagnostic_comparison>",
