    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Considered post-irradiation angiosarcoma as the most common PIS subtype — “angiosarcoma accounted for 56.8 % of the lesions…”\n2. Considered malignant fibrous histiocytoma given its relative PIS frequency — “malignant fibrous histiocytoma (MFH) accounting for 15.9 %…”\n3. Considered fibrosarcoma and chondrosarcoma among PIS subtypes — “fibrosarcoma and chondrosarcoma accounting for 6.8 % of the lesions…”\n4. Considered spindle-cell phyllodes tumor in the differential of breast spindle lesions — “The differential diagnosis of leiomyosarcoma includes various types of sarcoma, such as fibrosarcoma, MFH, and phyllodes tumor.”\n5. Considered primary leiomyosarcoma of the breast but noted extreme rarity versus post-irradiation origin given latency and field — “the lesion in our patient may have been primary leiomyosarcoma, and leiomyosarcoma of the breast is extremely rare.”\n6. Confirmed leiomyosarcoma based on smooth-muscle immunophenotype — “tumor cells were positive for α-SMA, desmin, and vimentin.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\nDiagnosis: Radiation-Induced Sarcoma\nEvidence: The patient has a history of radiation therapy to the left breast, which increases the risk of developing a radiation-induced sarcoma. The firm mass fixed to the pectoralis major muscle could be a sarcoma.\nEvidence: The patient received radiation therapy for her previous breast cancer, which increases the risk of developing a radiation-induced sarcoma. The new lump, which is fixed to the pectoralis major muscle, could be a sarcoma.\nEvidence: The patient has a history of radiation therapy, and the current lump is fixed to the pectoralis major muscle, which is a common site for radiation-induced sarcomas.\nDiagnosis: Breast Cancer Recurrence\nEvidence: The patient has a history of breast cancer and has undergone breast-conserving surgery, radiation, and endocrine therapy. The current lump in the lower outer quadrant of 