  "9a2202dc8d9226a45cff3681991c14f99c4551259eaf771a54e379e4c0afcbd3": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Restless arms syndrome — “These findings were consistent with RAS diagnosis, the most common RLS variant.”\n2. Akathisia — “Akathisia… does not worsen specifically at night or be temporarily alleviated through limb movements.”\n3. Augmentation — “Augmentation… can be ruled out in this case because the patient had never been treated with dopamine therapy before.”\n4. Escitalopram-induced RLS — “Escitalopram could be ruled out as the cause of RLS in our patient, as her symptoms persisted despite her discontinuing escitalopram.”\n5. Duloxetine-induced RLS — “RAS symptoms developed in response to duloxetine in a dose-dependent manner.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n- The patient has a 31-year history of recurrent depressive disorder, and her current symptoms, including depressed mood, anhedonia, nervousness, fatigue, decreased appetite, and poor sleep, are consistent with a major depressive episode.\n- The patient has a 31-year history of recurrent depressive disorder, and her current symptoms such as depressed mood, anhedonia, nervousness, fatigue, decreased appetite, and poor sleep are consistent with this diagnosis. The patient's history of responsiveness to escitalopram and duloxetine also supports this diagnosis.\n- The patient's symptoms of deep itching and creeping sensations in her bilateral shoulders and arms, worse at rest and partially relieved by movement, could also be related to restless legs syndrome, which is often associated with iron deficiency and can be exacerbated by antidepressants.\n- The patient's new symptoms of deep itching and creeping sensations in her bilateral shoulders and arms, worse at rest, partially relieved by movement, and predominantly occurring in the evening, are consistent with Restless Legs Syndrome. The patient's history of periodic limb movement disorder also increases the likelihood of this diagnosis.\n- The patient's new symptoms of deep itching and creeping sensations in her bilateral shoulders and arms, worse at rest and partially relieved by movement, are consistent with Restless Legs Syndrome. The patient's symptoms are also worse in the evening and cause nighttime sleep disturbance, which is typical of this condition.\n\nAnalysis for Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 1: Restless arms syndrome — “These findings were consistent with RAS diagnosis, the most common RLS variant.”\n- Potential match 1: \"The patient's symptoms of deep itching and creeping sensations in her bilateral shoulders and arms, worse at rest and partially relieved by movement, could also be related to restless legs syndrome, which is often associated with iron deficiency and can be exacerbated by antidepressants.\"\n    - Pros: This statement describes symptoms consistent with RLS/RAS (deep itching, creeping sensations, bilateral shoulders and arms, worse at rest, partially relieved by movement). It explicitly mentions \"restless legs syndrome,\" and RAS is a variant of RLS.\n    - Cons: It doesn't explicitly use the term \"Restless Arms Syndrome\" or state that it's the most common RLS variant.\n- Potential match 2: \"The patient's new symptoms of deep itching and creeping sensations in her bilateral shoulders and arms, worse at rest, partially relieved by movement, and predominantly occurring in the evening, are consistent with Restless Legs Syndrome. The patient's history of periodic limb movement disorder also increases the likelihood of this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: Similar to potential match 1, it describes symptoms consistent with RLS/RAS (deep itching, creeping sensations, bilateral shoulders and arms, worse at rest, partially relieved by movement). It explicitly mentions \"Restless Legs Syndrome.\"\n    - Cons: It doesn't explicitly use the term \"Restless Arms Syndrome\" or state that it's the most common RLS variant.\n- Potential match 3: \"The patient's new symptoms of deep itching and creeping sensations in her bilateral shoulders and arms, worse at rest and partially relieved by movement, are consistent with Restless Legs Syndrome. The patient's symptoms are also worse in the evening and cause nighttime sleep disturbance, which is typical of this condition.\"\n    - Pros: Similar to potential matches 1 and 2, it describes symptoms consistent with RLS/RAS (deep itching, creeping sensations, bilateral shoulders and arms, worse at rest, partially relieved by movement). It explicitly mentions \"Restless Legs Syndrome.\"\n    - Cons: It doesn't explicitly use the term \"Restless Arms Syndrome\" or state that it's the most common RLS variant.\n- Conclusion: All three predicted statements describe symptoms consistent with RLS, of which RAS is a variant. They all support the idea that the patient has a condition like RLS/RAS.\n\nAnalysis for Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 2: Akathisia — “Akathisia… does not worsen specifically at night or be temporarily alleviated through limb movements.”\n- No direct match. The predicted reasons focus on supporting RLS/depressive disorder, not ruling out akathisia.\n\nAnalysis for Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 3: Augmentation — “Augmentation… can be ruled out in this case because the patient had never been treated with dopamine therapy before.”\n- No direct match. The predicted reasons do not discuss augmentation or dopamine therapy.\n\nAnalysis for Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 4: Escitalopram-induced RLS — “Escitalopram could be ruled out as the cause of RLS in our patient, as her symptoms persisted despite her discontinuing escitalopram.”\n- No direct match. The predicted reasons mention escitalopram in the context of responsiveness for depressive disorder, but not in relation to RLS causation or discontinuation.\n\nAnalysis for Groundtruth Diagnostic Reason 5: Duloxetine-induced RLS — “RAS symptoms developed in response to duloxetine in a dose-dependent manner.”\n- Potential match 1: \"The patient's symptoms of deep itching and creeping sensations in her bilateral shoulders and arms, worse at rest and partially relieved by movement, could also be related to restless legs syndrome, which is often associated with iron deficiency and can be exacerbated by antidepressants.\"\n    - Pros: This statement mentions that RLS \"can be exacerbated by antidepressants.\" Duloxetine is an antidepressant. This provides a general link between antidepressants and RLS, which aligns with the ground truth that duloxetine induced RAS symptoms.\n    - Cons: It doesn't specifically name duloxetine or mention the dose-dependent manner. It's a general statement about antidepressants.\n- Potential match 2: \"The patient has a 31-year history of recurrent depressive disorder, and her current symptoms such as depressed mood, anhedonia, nervousness, fatigue, decreased appetite, and poor sleep are consistent with this diagnosis. The patient's history of responsiveness to escitalopram and duloxetine also supports this diagnosis.\"\n    - Pros: This statement explicitly mentions duloxetine.\n    - Cons: It mentions duloxetine in the context of treating depressive disorder, not as an inducer of RLS/RAS symptoms. It does not support the idea of duloxetine-induced RLS.\n- Conclusion: Potential match 1 is the closest, as it generally links antidepressants to RLS exacerbation, which is consistent with duloxetine (an antidepressant) inducing RAS symptoms.\n\nFinal Matching Decisions:\n1. For \"Restless arms syndrome — “These findings were consistent with RAS diagnosis, the most common RLS variant.”\", the matching statements are:\n    - \"The patient's symptoms of deep itching and creeping sensations in her bilateral shoulders and arms, worse at rest and partially relieved by movement, could also be related to restless legs syndrome, which is often associated with iron deficiency and can be exacerbated by antidepressants.\"\n    - \"The patient's new symptoms of deep itching and creeping sensations in her bilateral shoulders and arms, worse at rest, part