    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Facial nerve paralysis considered; excluded because other facial functions were intact — “Facial nerve lesions may cause weakness of facial expressions; however, congenital hypoplasia of depressor anguli oris afflicts lower lip while other functions of facial nerves are preserved.”\n2. Obstetric-related compression or trauma considered; excluded by lack of birth complications — “The child was born full-term with no complications to a mother without medical problems.”\n3. Syndromic cardiac anomalies (e.g., Cayler cardiofacial syndrome) considered; excluded by normal echocardiogram — “Echocardiogram did not show cardiac abnormality.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n- The patient's symptoms of unilateral lower lip depression during smiling or crying, with the face appearing symmetric at rest, are consistent with Asymmetric Crying Facies. This condition is a possible marker for congenital malformations and can be associated with various congenital abnormalities.\n- The patient's symptoms of unilateral lower lip depression during smiling or crying, with the face appearing symmetric at rest, are consistent with Asymmetric Crying Facies. This condition is a possible marker for congenital malformations and can be seen as an isolated clinical finding or associated with various congenital abnormalities.\n- The patient's presentation of facial asymmetry noted since birth, with the right lower lip deviating inward during smiling or crying, could be indicative of Congenital Facial Palsy. This condition can result from a variety of causes, including genetic factors or birth trauma.\n- The patient's presentation of facial asymmetry noted since birth, with the right lower lip deviating inward during smiling or crying, could be indicative of Congenital Facial Palsy. This condition involves partial paralysis of facial muscles that dates from birth and cannot be attributed to obstetric trauma.\n- The patient's