    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Cavernous angioma was initially suspected based on imaging — “suggesting the presence of a cavernous angioma.”\n2. Meningeal sarcoma with angiosarcomatous features was favored by pathology — “suggestive of meningeal sarcoma with angiosarcomatous features, ultimately assessed as a malignant endothelial cell sarcoma/AS.”\n3. Gliosarcoma was considered given the biphasic pattern of glial and mesenchymal differentiation — “Gliosarcoma ... was also considered. Further molecular analysis did not detect the presence of O6-methylguanine-DNA methyltransferase promoter methylation.”\n4. Metastatic malignancy was entertained due to a solitary pulmonary nodule on PET — “a concurrent PET scan showed a solitary pulmonary nodule assessed to be an infectious process or a malignancy.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n- Diagnosis: Solitary Fibrous Tumor/Hemangiopericytoma\n- Evidence: The patient's histopathological examination demonstrated spindle cells forming masses with branching ectatic vasculature, focal collagenous regions, and up to 20 mitoses per ten high-power fields. Immunohistochemical studies showed strong immunoreactivity for CD31, CD99, and Fli-1, with scattered S100-positive cells, and negative staining for CD34, EMA, desmin, muscle-specific actin, and Bcl-2. These findings are consistent with a Solitary Fibrous Tumor/Hemangiopericytoma.\n- Evidence: The patient's histopathological examination demonstrated spindle cells forming masses with branching ectatic vasculature, focal collagenous regions, and up to 20 mitoses per ten high-power fields, which is consistent with Solitary Fibrous Tumor/Hemangiopericytoma. The patient's immunohistochemical studies also showed strong immunoreactivity for CD31, CD99, and Fli-1, which further supports this diagnosis.\n- Diagnosis: Hemangiopericytoma\n- Evidence: The patient's histopathological examination demonstrated spindle cells forming masses with branchin