  "3e3e9e6c4a21078c26b1b19e7146afc37e1233735d7a318e2b461e7ef822123b": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Acute appendicitis was considered because of the migratory periumbilical to right lower quadrant pain and leukocytosis — “initial diagnosis of acute appendicitis.”\n2. Plastron appendicitis was entertained based on ultrasound findings — “Ultrasound assessment was compatible with plastron appendicitis.”\n3. Peptic ulcer perforation was less likely given the absence of free intraperitoneal air on X-ray — “there was no free air under the diaphragm at X-rays.”\n4. Foreign-body perforation was raised when CT demonstrated an intraluminal linear lesion causing partial obstruction and adjacent edema — “Computerized tomography (CT) scans revealed a linear lesion in the intestinal lumen of the right lower quadrant, causing a partial luminal obliteration and edema…”\n5. Other rare causes (urinary tract pathology, inflammatory bowel disease, intestinal parasitosis, malignancy, Meckel’s diverticulitis) were recognized as possibilities in the differential but were not supported by clinical or imaging findings — “Urinary tract pathologies, inflammatory bowel diseases, peptic ulcer perforations, intestinal parasitosis, malignancies, and Meckel’s diverticulitis should be considered in differential diagnosis of such a patient.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons (4 statements):\n- P1: \"Evidence: The patient's symptoms, including abdominal pain that began around the umbilicus and migrated to the right lower quadrant, are consistent with appendicitis. The Alvarado Clinical Scoring System, which is used to diagnose appendicitis, is also relevant to this case. The patient's laboratory results, including a white-cell count of 15,600/mL, also support this diagnosis.\"\n- P2: \"Evidence: The patient's symptoms, including abdominal pain that began around the umbilicus and migrated to the right lower quadrant, are consistent with appendicitis. The Alvarado Clinical Scoring System, which is used to diagnose appendicitis, is also relevant to this case. The patient's laboratory tests, including a white-cell count of 15,600/mL, also support this diagnosis.\"\n- P3: \"Evidence: The patient's symptoms, including abdominal pain and voluntary guarding in the right lower quadrant, could be consistent with intestinal obstruction. The patient's history of abdominal pain that began around the umbilicus and migrated to the right lower quadrant is also consistent with this diagnosis.\"\n- P4: \"Evidence: The patient's symptoms, including abdominal pain and voluntary guarding in the right lower quadrant, could be consistent with intestinal obstruction. The presence of several air-fluid levels on plain abdominal X-rays also supports this diagnosis.\"\n\nNow evaluating each groundtruth statement for matches:\n\nGroundtruth 1: Mentions migratory pain (periumbilical to RLQ) and leukocytosis (white-cell count). Predicted P1 and P2 both mention similar pain migration and white-cell count (15,600/mL). The groundtruth also mentions \"initial diagnosis of acute appendicitis\"; P1/P2 explicitly state symptoms are consistent with appendicitis. Pros: Core evidence matches. Cons: Groundtruth does not mention Alvarado score, but that is additional supportive detail, not contradictory. Therefore, P1 and P2 are both matches.\n\nGroundtruth 2: Mentions ultrasound findings for plastron appendicitis. None of the predicted statements mention ultrasound or plastron appendicitis. No match.\n\nGroundtruth 3: Mentions absence of free intraperitoneal air on X-ray. Predicted statements mention air-fluid levels (P4) but not the absence for peptic ulcer. No match.\n\nGroundtruth 4: Mentions CT finding of linear lesion causing partial obstruction. Predicted statements discuss intestinal obstruction but do not mention CT or linear lesion. No match.\n\nGroundtruth 5: Mentions other rare causes considered but not supported. Predicted statements do not discuss these differentials. No match.\n\nSummary of matches:\n- Groundtruth 1 matches P1 and P2.\n- Groundtruths 2,3,4,5 have no matches.\n\nNow constructing JSON output.\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [\n      \"Evidence: The patient's symptoms, including abdominal pain that began around the umbilicus and migrated to the right lower quadrant, are consistent with appendicitis. The Alvarado Clinical Scoring System, which is used to diagnose appendicitis, is also relevant to this case. The patient's laboratory results, including a white-cell count of 15,600/mL, also support this diagnosis.\",\n      \"Evidence: The patient's symptoms, including abdominal pain that began around the umbilicus and migrated to the right lower quadrant, are consistent with appendicitis. The Alvarado Clinical Scoring System, which is used to diagnose appendicitis, is also relevant to this case. The patient's laboratory tests, including a white-cell count of 15,600/mL, also support this diagnosis.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": [],\n    \"5\": []\n  }\n}\n```",
