  "365b4998e854fab5a83e7619f08e355988c59a8f55d2513202e76f15c1124bd5": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Consideration of a malignant ovarian tumor — “radiological findings were suggestive of an ovarian tumor; a staging laparotomy and surgical excision of the tumor and fibroid was planned with a provisional diagnosis of a malignant ovarian tumor with multiple fibroid and unilateral lymphedema.”\n2. Exclusion of dysgerminomatous and mixed germ cell tumors — “Radiological imaging in most of the situations can be ambiguous, however, with the addition of tumor markers, the differential diagnosis can be scaled down, differentiating this condition from dysgerminomatous and mixed germ cell tumors.”\n3. Secondary massive ovarian edema in benign tumors (e.g., mature cystic teratoma, fibrothecoma, Meigs syndrome) — “Secondary MOE can occur in benign ovarian tumors such as mature cystic teratoma, fibrothecoma, and Meigs syndrome, polycystic ovary, and metastatic malignancies…”\n4. Distinction from fibroma and luteinized thecoma — “The presence of preserved follicular structures within an edematous stroma can help differentiate the lesion from fibroma and luteinized thecoma.”\n5. Consideration of other edematous/myxoid ovarian neoplasms (e.g., sclerosing stromal tumor, Krukenberg tumor, luteinized thecoma with sclerosing peritonitis, ovarian myxoma) — “Any ovarian neoplasm that may exhibit edematous or myxoid appearance on the cut section can come in the differential diagnosis of ovarian edema, like fibroma, sclerosing stromal tumor, Krukenberg tumor, luteinized thecoma associated with sclerosing peritonitis, and ovarian myxoma.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n- Diagnosis: Ovarian Neoplasm\n  Evidence: The patient presents with a 1-month history of insidious-onset right lower abdominal pain and a large hypoechoic lesion in the right adnexa, which is concerning for an ovarian neoplasm. The presence of a firm, non-tender, mobile mass in the right lower quadrant and the imaging findings support this diagnosis.\n- Diagnosis: Malignant Ovarian Tumor\n  Evidence: The imaging findings, including a well-marginated thin-walled solid-cystic lesion with a solid component, are concerning for a malignant ovarian tumor. The patient's age and lack of other symptoms make this a plausible diagnosis.\n\nMatching analysis:\n\n1. Groundtruth statement 1 considers a malignant ovarian tumor based on radiological findings and provisional diagnosis.  \n   Potential match: “Diagnosis: Malignant Ovarian Tumor” and its evidence, because they explicitly refer to imaging findings concerning for a malignant ovarian tumor.  \n   Pros: Directly names malignant ovarian tumor; directly links it to imaging findings.  \n   Cons: None.  \n   The “Ovarian Neoplasm” statement was considered but is not an equivalent match because it does not address malignancy, which is the defining feature of groundtruth statement 1.\n\n2. Groundtruth statement 2 discusses exclusion of dysgerminomatous and mixed germ cell tumors using tumor markers.  \n   No predicted statement mentions tumor markers, dysgerminoma, mixed germ cell tumors, or a differential diagnosis using tumor markers.  \n   No match.\n\n3. Groundtruth statement 3 discusses secondary massive ovarian edema in benign tumors such as mature cystic teratoma, fibrothecoma, and Meigs syndrome.  \n   No predicted statement mentions massive ovarian edema, benign tumors, mature cystic teratoma, fibrothecoma, or Meigs syndrome.  \n   No match.\n\n4. Groundtruth statement 4 discusses distinction from fibroma and luteinized thecoma using preserved follicular structures.  \n   No predicted statement mentions fibroma, luteinized thecoma, or preserved follicular structures.  \n   No match.\n\n5. Groundtruth statement 5 discusses other edematous/myxoid ovarian neoplasms such as sclerosing stromal tumor, Krukenberg tumor, luteinized thecoma with sclerosing peritonitis, and ovarian myxoma.  \n   No predicted statement mentions these entities or the edematous/myxoid differential diagnosis.  \n   No match.\n\nFinal matching decisions:\n- Statement 1 matches “Diagnosis: Malignant Ovarian Tumor” and its associated evidence.\n- Statements 2, 3, 4, and 5 have no matches in the predicted diagnostic reasons.\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [\n      \"Diagnosis: Malignant Ovarian Tumor\",\n      \"The imaging findings, including a well-marginated thin-walled solid-cystic lesion with a solid component, are concerning for a malignant ovarian tumor. The patient's age and lack of other symptoms make this a plausible diagnosis.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": [],\n    \"5\": []\n  }\n}\n```",
    "prompt_id": "mcr.reasoning_recall"
  },
  "f2ac631d46553d66509c7188f41f89b11e63c0fa30a72feea4c3065e724e5187": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Ovarian cancer was suspected because of adnexal thickening and elevated CA-125 — “The radiologist suspected primary malignancy originating from thickening of bilateral adnexa which could be in the form of ovarian cancer.”\n2. A gynecologic origin was less likely after ultrasound showed no ovarian abnormalities — “There were no signs of abnormalities such as cancer or cysts on gynecological organs based on the USG.”\n3. Bone metastases were contemplated due to lytic, hypermetabolic bone lesions — “multiple osteolytic hypermetabolic metastases located in bone marrow of right humerus head, sternal bone region, several bilateral ribs, pelvic bone, and most of cervical/sacral vertebra bone.”\n4. Peritoneal carcinomatosis was considered from omental changes on PET — “presence of non-metabolic bleakness at the area of upper omentum with suspicion of metastatic seeding in the form of carcinomatosis omental.”\n5. Malignancy was questioned when fluid cytology showed no malignant cells — “Cytologic examination of pleural fluid showed lymphocytes, leukocytes, and mesothelial cells with no presence of malignant cells.”\n6. Tuberculous lymphadenitis was identified on lymph-node biopsy — “the biopsy showed tubercles surrounded by lymphocytes... the pathological anatomy report concluded the diagnosis as tuberculous lymphadenitis.”\n7. Abdominal tuberculosis was confirmed by laparoscopy and biopsy showing granulomas — “Omentum biopsy revealed granuloma containing epithelioid cells and Langhans-type cells. These results suggested tuberculosis infection of omentum and liver.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons (list of evidence statements):\nP1: \"The patient's symptoms of worsening dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, and significant weight loss, along with the findings of bilateral pleural effusions, pericardial effusion, and lytic lesions in the sternum, right humerus, and thoracic vertebrae, are highly suggestive of a malignant process. The elevated CA-125 level and the PET-CT findings of FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa suspicious for primary ovarian malignancy support this diagnosis.\"\nP2: \"The patient's symptoms, such as dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, and weight loss, along with the PET-CT findings of FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa suspicious for primary ovarian malignancy, suggest ovarian cancer as a possible diagnosis.\"\nP3: \"The patient's symptoms, such as worsening dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, and significant weight loss, along with laboratory findings like elevated CA-125 and imaging results showing bilateral pleural and pericardial effusions, are highly suggestive of ovarian cancer. The PET-CT findings of FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa further support this diagnosis.\"\nP4: \"The PET-CT findings of hypermetabolic osteolytic lesions in the bone, along with the patient's symptoms and the presence of a possible primary ovarian malignancy, suggest metastatic ovarian cancer to the bone as a possible diagnosis.\"\nP5: \"Given the patient's presentation and the imaging findings, including lytic lesions in the sternum, right humerus, and thoracic vertebrae, metastatic ovarian cancer to the bone is a plausible diagnosis. The presence of hypermetabolic osteolytic lesions in bone on PET-CT also supports this diagnosis.\"\n\nMatching analysis for each groundtruth statement:\n\nGroundtruth 1: Ovarian cancer suspected due to adnexal thickening and elevated CA-125.\n- Potential matches: P1, P2, P3 all mention either FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa (P1,P2,P3) and/or elevated CA-125 (P1,P3). These directly state suspicion of ovarian cancer based on those findings. P4 and P5 mention \"possible primary ovarian malignancy\" but do not specify adnexal thickening or CA-125; they are less specific and not a direct match.\n- Pros: P1,P2,P3 contain the exact concepts (adnexal thickening, CA-125) and link to ovarian cancer. Cons: None.\n- Decision: Match P1, P2, P3.\n\nGroundtruth 2: Gynecologic origin less likely after ultrasound showed no ovarian abnormalities.\n- Potential matches: No predicted statement mentions ultrasound or normal gynecologic organs. All predicted statements assume ovarian cancer as a primary diagnosis, not that it was ruled out.\n- Pros: None. Cons: No mention.\n- Decision: No match.\n\nGroundtruth 3: Bone metastases contemplated due to lytic, hypermetabolic bone lesions.\n- Potential matches: P1 mentions \"lytic lesions in the sternum, right humerus, and thoracic vertebrae\". P4 mentions \"hypermetabolic osteolytic lesions in the bone\" and \"metastatic ovarian cancer to the bone\". P5 mentions \"lytic lesions\" and \"hypermetabolic osteolytic lesions\" and \"metastatic ovarian cancer to the bone\". All directly address the presence of lytic/hypermetabolic bone lesions and consider bone metastases. P2 and P3 do not mention bone lesions.\n- Pros: P1,P4,P5 all include the key findings. Cons: P1 does not explicitly say \"bone metastases\" but implies malignant process; however, the context of lytic lesions in bones is clearly part of contemplating bone metastases. Acceptable.\n- Decision: Match P1, P4, P5.\n\nGroundtruth 4: Peritoneal carcinomatosis considered from omental changes on PET.\n- Potential matches: No predicted statement mentions omentum, peritoneal carcinomatosis, or omental changes. All focus on adnexal, bone, or pleural/pericardial effusions.\n- Pros: None. Cons: No mention.\n- Decision: No match.\n\nGroundtruth 5: Malignancy questioned when fluid cytology showed no malignant cells.\n- Potential matches: No predicted statement mentions pleural fluid cytology or the absence of malignant cells. They mention pleural effusions but not cytology results.\n- Pros: None. Cons: No mention.\n- Decision: No match.\n\nGroundtruth 6: Tuberculous lymphadenitis identified on lymph-node biopsy.\n- Potential matches: No predicted statement mentions lymph-node biopsy, tubercles, or tuberculous lymphadenitis. All focus on cancer.\n- Pros: None. Cons: No mention.\n- Decision: No match.\n\nGroundtruth 7: Abdominal tuberculosis confirmed by laparoscopy and biopsy showing granulomas.\n- Potential matches: No predicted statement mentions tuberculosis, granulomas, omentum biopsy, or laparoscopy.\n- Pros: None. Cons: No mention.\n- Decision: No match.\n\nSummary of matching decisions:\n- Groundtruth 1 matches P1, P2, P3.\n- Groundtruth 3 matches P1, P4, P5.\n- All other groundtruth statements have no matches.\n</diagnostic_comparison>\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [\n      \"The patient's symptoms of worsening dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, and significant weight loss, along with the findings of bilateral pleural effusions, pericardial effusion, and lytic lesions in the sternum, right humerus, and thoracic vertebrae, are highly suggestive of a malignant process. The elevated CA-125 level and the PET-CT findings of FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa suspicious for primary ovarian malignancy support this diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient's symptoms, such as dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, and weight loss, along with the PET-CT findings of FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa suspicious for primary ovarian malignancy, suggest ovarian cancer as a possible diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient's symptoms, such as worsening dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, and significant weight loss, along with laboratory findings like elevated CA-125 and imaging results showing bilateral pleural and pericardial effusions, are highly suggestive of ovarian cancer. The PET-CT findings of FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa further support this diagnosis.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [\n      \"The patient's symptoms of worsening dyspnea, dry cough, fatigue, and significant weight loss, along with the findings of bilateral pleural effusions, pericardial effusion, and lytic lesions in the sternum, right humerus, and thoracic vertebrae, are highly suggestive of a malignant process. The elevated CA-125 level and the PET-CT findings of FDG-avid thickening of bilateral adnexa suspicious for primary ovarian malignancy support this diagnosis.\",\n      \"The PET-CT findings of hypermetabolic osteolytic lesions in the bone, along with the patient's symptoms and the presence of a possible primary ovarian malignancy, suggest metastatic ovarian cancer to the bone as a possible diagnosis.\",\n      \"Given the patient's presentation and the imaging findings, including lytic lesions in the sternum, right humerus, and thoracic vertebrae, metastatic ovarian cancer to the bone is a plausible diagnosis. The presence of hypermetabolic osteolytic lesions in bone on PET-CT also supports this diagnosis.\"\n    ],\n    \"4\": [],\n    \"5\": [],\n    \"6\": [],\n    \"7\": []\n  }\n}\n```",
    "prompt_id": "mcr.reasoning_recall"
  },
