  "0caaf06fb6a168d418d4d1c4440801a684489aa3fba45508ac7b1be0f6a63e7f": {
    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\nGroundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n1. Upper urinary tract urothelial carcinoma was suspected because “Typical symptoms are gross hematuria (80%) and flank pain (30%)” and imaging showed “a tubular filling defect with partial obstruction of the right mid ureter.”\n2. Ureteral fibroepithelial polyp was considered given that “IVU or retrograde pyelography typically demonstrates a tubular filling defect. This finding combined with negative cytology and biopsy should suspect a UFP.”\n3. Other benign ureteral lesions, including endometriomas, fibromas, leiomyomas, granulomas, neurofibromas, hemangiomas, and lymphangiomas, were contemplated because “Other benign lesions of the upper urinary tract include endometriomas, fibromas, leiomyomas, granulomas, neurofibromas, hemangiomas, and lymphangiomas.”\n\nPredicted Diagnostic Reasons:\nP1: \"The patient presented with gross hematuria and intermittent right pelvic pain. Imaging studies, including intravenous urography and retrograde ureteropyelography, revealed a tubular filling defect with partial obstruction of the right mid ureter, and flexible ureteroscopy showed a pedunculated ureteral tumor with a vulnerable surface. Although urine cytology and endoscopic biopsy were negative for malignancy, the clinical and imaging findings are consistent with a ureteral tumor.\"\nP2: \"The patient presented with gross hematuria and intermittent right pelvic pain, and imaging studies revealed a tubular filling defect with partial obstruction of the right mid ureter, which is consistent with a ureteral tumor.\"\nP3: \"The patient's age, smoking history, and presentation with gross hematuria and intermittent right pelvic pain increase the suspicion for urothelial carcinoma. Although the biopsy results were negative, the clinical and imaging findings, including the tubular filling defect and partial obstruction of the right mid ureter, could be consistent with a urothelial carcinoma.\"\nP4: \"The patient's symptoms and imaging findings are consistent with a urothelial carcinoma, which is a type of cancer that can occur in the ureters, and the patient's 60-pack-year smoking history increases the risk of this diagnosis.\"\nP5: \"The patient's symptoms and imaging findings are consistent with a urothelial carcinoma, which is a type of cancer that can occur in the ureter.\"\n\nMatching analysis:\n\nGroundtruth 1:\n- P3 is a strong match: it explicitly mentions gross hematuria, pain, tubular filling defect, partial obstruction of the right mid ureter, and consistency with urothelial carcinoma.\n- P4 and P5 are also matches: they state that the patient's symptoms and imaging findings are consistent with urothelial carcinoma in the ureter/ureters, which aligns with the suspected upper urinary tract urothelial carcinoma.\n- P1 and P2 are not ideal matches because they conclude \"ureteral tumor\" rather than specifically supporting urothelial carcinoma.\n\nGroundtruth 2:\n- No predicted statement mentions ureteral fibroepithelial polyp or uses the combination of tubular filling defect with negative cytology and biopsy to suspect a UFP.\n- P1 mentions negative cytology/biopsy and tubular filling defect, but it concludes \"ureteral tumor,\" not UFP.\n- Therefore, no match.\n\nGroundtruth 3:\n- No predicted statement mentions the other benign ureteral lesions listed, such as endometriomas, fibromas, leiomyomas, granulomas, neurofibromas, hemangiomas, or lymphangiomas.\n- Therefore, no match.\n\nFinal matching decisions:\n- Groundtruth 1 matches P3, P4, and P5.\n- Groundtruth 2 has no match.\n- Groundtruth 3 has no match.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [\n      \"The patient's age, smoking history, and presentation with gross hematuria and intermittent right pelvic pain increase the suspicion for urothelial carcinoma. Although the biopsy results were negative, the clinical and imaging findings, including the tubular filling defect and partial obstruction of the right mid ureter, could be consistent with a urothelial carcinoma.\",\n      \"The patient's symptoms and imaging findings are consistent with a urothelial carcinoma, which is a type of cancer that can occur in the ureters, and the patient's 60-pack-year smoking history increases the risk of this diagnosis.\",\n      \"The patient's symptoms and imaging findings are consistent with a urothelial carcinoma, which is a type of cancer that can occur in the ureter.\"\n    ],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": []\n  }\n}\n```",
    "prompt_id": "mcr.reasoning_recall"
  },
