    "text": "<diagnostic_comparison>\n1. Groundtruth Diagnostic Reasons:\n   - G1: Consideration of urinoma—“Multiple slightly strong echoes, septations, and flocculent echoes were more likely to be seen in ultrasonography of hematoma.”\n   - G2: Inclusion of lymphangioma and mesenteric cyst—“The differential diagnosis of cystic masses located between the kidney and spine includes urinoma, lymphangioma, mesenteric cyst, and so on.”\n   - G3: Exclusion of renal tumor (eg, angiomyolipoma)—“Literature research indicated that renal tumors were the most common cause of WS, and hydronephrosis was an uncommon cause.”\n   - G4: Identification of hydronephrosis secondary to bladder dysfunction as the hemorrhage cause—“In the present case, hydronephrosis was believed to be the direct cause of WS.”\n\n2. Predicted Diagnostic Reasons:\n   - P1: \"The prenatal ultrasound findings of bilateral renal enlargement and hydronephrosis, a markedly dilated fetal bladder, and a left perirenal fluid collection are consistent with posterior urethral valves, a condition that causes obstructive uropathy.\"\n   - P2: \"The prenatal ultrasound showed a markedly dilated fetal bladder and bilateral renal enlargement and hydronephrosis, which are consistent with posterior urethral valves. The postnatal MRI and magnetic resonance urography also revealed bladder wall irregularity and thickening, and bilateral ureteral dilation, supporting this diagnosis.\"\n   - P3: \"The prenatal ultrasound showed bilateral renal enlargement and hydronephrosis, a markedly dilated fetal bladder, and a left perirenal fluid collection. These findings are consistent with posterior urethral valves, which can cause obstructive uropathy and subsequent renal impairment.\"\n   - P4: \"The combination of bilateral renal enlargement and hydronephrosis, a dilated bladder, and a perirenal fluid collection could also suggest prune belly syndrome, a rare congenital disorder characterized by absent or weakened abdominal muscles, undescended testes, and urinary tract anomalies.\"\n   - P5: \"The prenatal ultrasound showed bilateral renal enlargement and hydronephrosis, and a left perirenal fluid collection, which could be consistent with prune belly syndrome. The postnatal physical examination was normal, but the syndrome can have variable presentations.\"\n   - P6: \"The combination of bilateral renal enlargement and hydronephrosis, a dilated bladder, and a perirenal fluid collection is also suggestive of prune belly syndrome, a rare congenital disorder characterized by absent or weakened abdominal muscles, undescended testes, and urinary tract anomalies.\"\n\n3. Matching analysis:\n   - For G1: No predicted statement mentions urinoma, hematoma, septations, flocculent echoes, or ultrasonographic features of a hematoma. No match.\n   - For G2: No predicted statement mentions lymphangioma, mesenteric cyst, or differential diagnosis of cystic masses between the kidney and spine. No match.\n   - For G3: No predicted statement mentions renal tumor, angiomyolipoma, WS, or the relative frequency of renal tumors versus hydronephrosis as causes. No match.\n   - For G4: No predicted statement identifies hydronephrosis as a direct cause of hemorrhage or WS. Predicted statements mention hydronephrosis as a finding associated with posterior urethral valves and prune belly syndrome, but not as the hemorrhagic cause in this case. No match.\n\n4. Final matching decisions:\n   - G1: no matching predicted statements\n   - G2: no matching predicted statements\n   - G3: no matching predicted statements\n   - G4: no matching predicted statements\n\n```json\n{\n  \"matching_dict\": {\n    \"1\": [],\n    \"2\": [],\n    \"3\": [],\n    \"4\": []\n  }\n}\n```",
